    "q-143258": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143258",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\le 21),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20} & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(20,80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\le 21).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 21) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{21-20}{\\sqrt{80}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{80}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.11) \\\\  & =Φ(0.11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.11)=0.5438.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 21)=Φ(0.11)=0.5438.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
