    "q-152019": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152019",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "In a particular shop, cables are sold in coils. The length of the cable in a coil is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{m}.$ If John bought $5$ coils, what is the probability that the total length of the cables he bought is greater than $250\\,\\text{m}$ and less than $253\\,m?$ You may assume that the lengths of the cable in the coils are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9554\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9641\\,$ | $\\,0.9649\\,$ | $\\,0.9656\\,$ | $\\,0.9664\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the length of cable in the $i$th coil. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(50,{0.7}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 5.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $L$ denote the total length of the cables in $5$ randomly selected coils. Then, since\n\n$$\nL={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{5},\n$$\n\nwe have that $L$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[L] & =5\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =250, \\\\ \\text{Var}[L] & =5\\cdot {0.7}^{2} \\\\  & =2.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL∼N(250,2.45).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(250<L<253),$ we convert $L$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(250<L<253) & =P(\\frac{250-250}{\\sqrt{2.45}}<Z<\\frac{253-250}{\\sqrt{2.45}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(1.92)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.92)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.92)≈0.9726.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(250<L<253) & ≈Φ(0.95)-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.4726.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
