    "q-152022": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152022",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Fred picked $10$ watermelons from his vegetable garden. The weight of watermelons he grows is normally distributed with a mean of $11\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $3\\,\\text{kg}.$ What is the probability that the total weight of the watermelons Fred picked is greater than $100\\,\\text{kg}$ and less than $110\\,\\text{kg}?$ You may assume that the weights of the watermelons are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th watermelon. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(11,{3}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 10.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denotes the total weight of the $10$ watermelons. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =10\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =110, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =10\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(110,90).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(100<W<110),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<W<110) & =P(\\frac{100-110}{\\sqrt{90}}<Z<\\frac{110-110}{\\sqrt{90}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.05<Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-1.05) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-(1-Φ(1.05)) \\\\  & =0.5-(1-Φ(1.05)) \\\\  & =Φ(1.05)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.05)≈0.8531.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<W<110) & ≈Φ(1.05)-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.3531.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
